-- Q6 [Enzymes as Catalysts] hard skill2 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Enzymes as Catalysts', 'A reaction energy diagram for an uncatalyzed reaction labels two arrows along the reaction coordinate: arrow X runs from the reactant level up to the peak of the energy barrier, and arrow Y runs from the reactant level down to the lower product level. A researcher then adds a catalytic enzyme and re-plots the diagram for the same reactants and products. Which arrow is shortened by the enzyme, and which is unchanged?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Arrow Y shortens; arrow X unchanged"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Arrow X shortens; arrow Y unchanged"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Both arrows shorten by the same amount"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Arrow X shortens; arrow Y lengthens"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is B because arrow X (reactant level to the peak) represents the activation energy \(E_a\), while arrow Y (reactant level to product level) represents the overall \(\Delta G\). An enzyme is a catalyst that lowers the activation energy by stabilizing the transition state, so for the reaction to be catalyzed the activation energy in the presence of the enzyme must be "less positive (i.e., smaller) than the activation energy ... in the absence of the enzyme" (LibreTexts 25597-25598); thus arrow X shortens. The textbook is explicit that "Enzymes do not affect the thermodynamics of reactions" and that for reversible reactions "the equilibrium constant, Keq, is unchanged" — only "the rate at which equilibrium is achieved" changes (LibreTexts 26826-26828). Because \(\Delta G\) is a thermodynamic quantity set only by the reactant and product energy levels, arrow Y is unchanged. (Choice A) reverses the assignment: it shrinks the \(\Delta G\) arrow and leaves \(E_a\) untouched, but enzymes lower \(E_a\), not \(\Delta G\) (LibreTexts 26826-26828). (Choice B) is correct: the enzyme lowers \(E_a\) (arrow X) while leaving \(\Delta G\) (arrow Y) unchanged. (Choice C) is a partial truth — the enzyme does lower the energy of the barrier, but it does NOT lower \(\Delta G\); the diagram''s reactant and product levels are fixed, so the two arrows cannot shrink equally (LibreTexts 27698-27699). (Choice D) correctly lowers the barrier (arrow X) but wrongly claims the enzyme also drives the product level lower, lengthening arrow Y; the reactant and product free-energy levels that fix \(\Delta G\) are unchanged, so arrow Y neither lengthens nor shortens (LibreTexts 26826-26828). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the principle that catalysts lower \(E_a\) but not \(\Delta G\) to interpret which labeled arrow on the energy diagram changes.', 'hard', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 150)
  RETURNING id
)
