-- Q7 [Enzymes as Catalysts] hard skill1 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Enzymes as Catalysts', 'A pre-med student is comparing how a single enzyme acts on two different cellular reactions: an exergonic step and an endergonic step. While reviewing the underlying thermodynamics, the student records the statements below.

I. The exergonic reaction has \(\Delta G<0\).
II. The enzyme accelerates the exergonic reaction without altering its \(\Delta G\).
III. The enzyme converts the endergonic reaction into an exergonic one.

Which of the statements are correct?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "I only"}, {"label": "B", "text": "III only"}, {"label": "C", "text": "I and III only"}, {"label": "D", "text": "I and II only"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is D because statements I and II are both true, while statement III is false. An exergonic reaction is defined by a negative free-energy change: "reactions that release energy have a ∆G < 0... Scientists call reactions that have a negative ∆G and consequently release free energy exergonic reactions" (OpenStax Biology 2e, lines 8085-8089), confirming statement I. An enzyme is "a biological catalyst that speeds up chemical reactions" (LibreTexts Biochemistry, line 1259) by lowering the activation energy, yet "enzymes do not change the reaction''s ∆G... They only reduce the activation energy required to reach the transition state" (OpenStax Biology 2e, lines 8457-8460), confirming statement II. Statement III is false because that same passage states enzymes "do not change whether a reaction is exergonic (spontaneous) or endergonic" (OpenStax Biology 2e, lines 8458-8459); an enzyme changes only how fast equilibrium is reached, never the sign of \(\Delta G\), so it cannot make an endergonic reaction exergonic. (Choice A) is wrong because it omits statement II, which is correctly supported by the textbook''s point that catalysis leaves \(\Delta G\) unchanged. (Choice B) is wrong because it selects only the false statement III and discards the two true statements. (Choice C) is wrong because it pairs the true statement I with the false statement III, the partial-truth trap. (Choice D) is correct: statements I and II are both supported by the textbook, and statement III is excluded because an enzyme cannot alter the thermodynamic favorability (\(\Delta G\)) of a reaction. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the definitions of exergonic and endergonic reactions and the principle that enzymes lower activation energy without changing \(\Delta G\).', 'hard', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 1, 150)
  RETURNING id
)
