-- Q10 [Enzyme Classification (EC Classes)] easy skill1 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Enzyme Classification (EC Classes)', 'Hexokinase catalyzes the first step of glycolysis, in which the terminal phosphate of \(\text{ATP}\) is covalently transferred onto a glucose molecule to form glucose-6-phosphate. Based on the reaction it catalyzes, hexokinase is best classified as which of the following enzymes?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Hydrolase"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ligase"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Isomerase"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Transferase"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is D because the Enzyme Commission classifies enzymes by the type of reaction they catalyze, and an enzyme that moves a functional group from one molecule to another is a transferase. The textbook explicitly assigns "hexokinase (EC 2.7.1.1)," whose leading digit ''2'' is the transferase class, and states that "A protein kinase is an enzyme that covalently transfers a terminal phosphate (P) from ATP to a protein," while the post-translational modification figure groups the phosphate-adding "kinases" with the "transferases." Because hexokinase transfers the terminal phosphate of \(\text{ATP}\) onto glucose, it moves a functional group rather than breaking, joining, or rearranging the molecule, so it is a transferase. (Choice A) is incorrect: a hydrolase breaks a bond by adding water; in the textbook figure it is the phosphatases, not the kinases, that remove phosphate using \(\text{H}_2\text{O}\), so confusing the phosphate-adding step with the water-using removal step misassigns the class. (Choice B) is incorrect: a ligase also uses ATP, but it joins two separate molecules into a larger one; hexokinase does not ligate two molecules together, it transfers only the phosphate group, so the shared ATP requirement is a tempting but distinct feature. (Choice C) is incorrect: an isomerase rearranges bonds within a single molecule to form an isomer and adds nothing from another molecule, whereas hexokinase adds a phosphate group brought in from ATP. (Choice D) is correct: the enzyme transfers the terminal phosphate of ATP onto a substrate, the defining action of a transferase (the class that includes kinases), and the textbook lists hexokinase as EC 2.7.1.1. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the defining reaction of each EC class and match the described phosphate-transfer reaction to the transferase class.', 'easy', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 1, 75)
  RETURNING id
)
