-- Q11 [Enzyme Classification (EC Classes)] medium skill2 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Enzyme Classification (EC Classes)', 'A purified digestive enzyme acts on a polypeptide substrate. Tracking the reaction, a researcher finds that the enzyme inserts the atoms of a single water molecule across one peptide bond, splitting the chain into two shorter fragments — one gaining a new carboxyl group and the other a new amine — with no electrons transferred and no functional group shuttled to a second molecule. To which Enzyme Commission (EC) class does this enzyme belong?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Lyase"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Transferase"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Hydrolase"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Oxidoreductase"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is C because the enzyme breaks a covalent bond (here, a peptide/amide bond) by adding the atoms of water across it — the defining reaction of a hydrolase. LibreTexts states that for the peptide bond, "the reverse reaction, hydrolysis, requires the incorporation of a water molecule to separate two amino acids and break the amide bond... a class of enzymes called proteases catalyzes protein hydrolysis," and the IUBMB Enzyme Commission table lists "Hydrolases [EC 3]" with the reaction description "hydrolysis reactions." Because a water molecule is consumed to cleave the bond — with no redox and no group transferred to a second molecule — the enzyme is a hydrolase. (Choice A) Lyases are "[EC 4]" enzymes characterized by "elimination forming a double bond" — they cleave bonds WITHOUT adding water, the opposite of what is described. (Choice B) Transferases are "[EC 2]" enzymes that catalyze the "transfer/exchange of group from one molecule to another," but here no group is moved to a second molecule — the chain is simply split by water. (Choice C) is correct: the reaction is bond cleavage by addition of water (hydrolysis), the EC 3 hydrolase reaction, exactly as a protease performs on a peptide bond. (Choice D) Oxidoreductases are "[EC 1]" enzymes that catalyze "redox reactions" (electron transfer); the stem explicitly states no electrons are transferred. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the EC classification scheme to a described reaction and reason from the mechanistic clues (water added, bond broken, no redox, no group transfer) to assign the correct class.', 'medium', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 105)
  RETURNING id
)
