-- Q13 [Enzyme Classification (EC Classes)] medium skill2 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Enzyme Classification (EC Classes)', 'A purified enzyme covalently joins a free fatty acid to coenzyme A, combining the two separate substrates into a single acyl-CoA product. Each catalytic cycle consumes one molecule of \(\text{ATP}\), whose hydrolysis supplies the energy that drives formation of the new covalent bond. Based on the reaction it catalyzes, this enzyme is classified as a:', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Ligase"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Lyase"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Transferase"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Hydrolase"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is A because the enzyme joins two separate molecules into one larger product and requires the energy of ATP hydrolysis to drive formation of the new covalent bond — the defining feature of a ligase. LibreTexts describes exactly this energetic logic for fatty acid-CoA formation, noting it is "an endergonic reaction powered by ATP" (LibreTexts 47664-47665), and explains that forming such a bond "requires activating" one end via "the transfer of a phosphate from a phosphoanhydride, ATP" so that bond formation can proceed (LibreTexts 6406-6407). Because the diagnostic clues are (1) two molecules being joined into one product and (2) an obligatory ATP requirement, the reaction maps onto the ligase class rather than any class that merely rearranges, transfers, or cleaves. (Choice A) is correct: a ligase synthesizes one larger molecule from two, using ATP hydrolysis to power the new bond (LibreTexts 6406-6407, 47664-47665). (Choice B) is wrong by a reversed relationship: a lyase does the opposite — it CLEAVES a single molecule into two without adding water and without ATP (the textbook''s example enzyme is hydroxynitrile lyase, LibreTexts 6485), so it neither joins molecules nor consumes ATP. (Choice C) is a tempting adjacent fact: transferases move a functional group from one molecule to another (e.g., glycosyl and lipid transferases, LibreTexts 6258, 6271; kinases such as hexokinase transfer a phosphate, LibreTexts 28956), but they do not fuse two substrates into a single product nor require net ATP-driven bond synthesis. (Choice D) confuses the energy source with the enzyme class: "ATP hydrolysis" names the side reaction that powers the bond, but a hydrolase is defined by breaking a bond by ADDING WATER, and enzymes "do not change the reactants'' or products'' free energy" by coupling to ATP — they cleave substrates, the opposite of joining them (OpenStax 8457-8459). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the EC classification rules to a described reaction to deduce the enzyme''s class.', 'medium', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 105)
  RETURNING id
)
