-- Q14 [Enzyme Classification (EC Classes)] medium skill2 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Enzyme Classification (EC Classes)', 'During glycolysis, one enzyme splits fructose-1,6-bisphosphate into two three-carbon products, dihydroxyacetone phosphate and glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate, in a single step that consumes NO water and involves NO transfer of electrons between the substrate and any acceptor. Into which EC class does this enzyme fall?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Hydrolase"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Lyase"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Oxidoreductase"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Ligase"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is B because a lyase cleaves a single molecule into two products without using water and without a redox change (or runs the reverse, adding a group across a double bond). The described enzyme is aldolase, which in glycolysis Step 4 acts "to cleave fructose-1,6-bisphosphate into two three-carbon isomers: dihydroxyacetone phosphate and glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate" (OpenStax Biology 2e, line 9252). Because the stem specifies that the split consumes no water and transfers no electrons, the two water-dependent and redox-dependent classes are ruled out, leaving the non-hydrolytic, non-oxidative cleavage characteristic of a lyase (cf. hydroxynitrile lyase, LibreTexts Biochemistry lines 6376, 6532). (Choice A) is incorrect because a hydrolase breaks a bond by adding water, as when "the disaccharide maltose breaks down to form two glucose monomers by adding a water molecule" (OpenStax 2e, line 3681); the stem states no water is consumed. (Choice B) is correct: the enzyme cleaves one molecule into two products with neither water nor electron transfer, which defines a lyase. (Choice C) is incorrect because an oxidoreductase catalyzes oxidation-reduction (redox) reactions, the "movement of electrons from one element to another" (OpenStax 2e, lines 9000, 3399); the stem rules out any electron transfer. (Choice D) is incorrect because a ligase joins smaller molecules into a larger one, as when "ligases join them back together" (LibreTexts Biochemistry, line 42087), the reverse of the cleavage described here. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the defining criteria of the six EC classes to a described reaction and assign the correct class.', 'medium', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 105)
  RETURNING id
)
