-- Q15 [Enzyme Classification (EC Classes)] hard skill2 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Enzyme Classification (EC Classes)', 'A liver enzyme catalyzes a reaction in which a substrate molecule is oxidized: two electrons and a proton are stripped from the substrate and delivered to a bound \(\text{NAD}^+\) cofactor, converting it to \(\text{NADH}\) as the substrate is converted to its product. Based on the chemistry described, this enzyme belongs to which EC class?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Oxidoreductase"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Transferase"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Hydrolase"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Isomerase"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is A because the defining event of the described reaction is a redox transfer: electrons are removed from the substrate (it is oxidized) and added to \(\text{NAD}^+\) (it is reduced to \(\text{NADH}\)). Enzymes that catalyze oxidation–reduction by moving electrons from a donor to an acceptor are oxidoreductases, the first of the six EC classes. The textbook states that "when electrons are removed from a compound, it is oxidized" and "when electrons are added to a compound, it is reduced," with \(\text{NAD}^+\) acting as the electron-accepting carrier that becomes \(\text{NADH}\) (OpenStax Biology 2e, lines 9046-9049, 9059-9060); the EC scheme assigns class 1 to oxidoreductases (LibreTexts Biochemistry, line 17898). (Choice A) is correct: a reaction whose chemistry is the transfer of electrons from a donor substrate to an acceptor cofactor is, by definition, catalyzed by an oxidoreductase. (Choice B) is tempting because electrons (as a hydride) do move from substrate to cofactor, and transferases "transfer a functional group"; but a transferase moves an intact chemical group (e.g., a methyl or phosphate) between molecules without any change in oxidation state, as in the methyltransferase that hands off a methyl group (LibreTexts, lines 28773, 28791), not bare electrons in a redox event. (Choice C) is wrong because a hydrolase cleaves a bond by adding water (hydrolysis), and no water is consumed here (LibreTexts, line 29474; OpenStax, lines 3670-3671). (Choice D) is wrong because an isomerase rearranges bonds within a single molecule to interconvert isomers with no net oxidation, reduction, or second substrate, as triose phosphate isomerase does (LibreTexts, lines 26117, 25130). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the definitions of the EC classes to a described reaction mechanism and identify the redox event as the diagnostic feature.', 'hard', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 150)
  RETURNING id
)
