-- Q17 [Enzyme Classification (EC Classes)] hard skill2 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Enzyme Classification (EC Classes)', 'A biochemist characterizes three enzyme-catalyzed reactions in a cell extract: (I) a peptidase splits a peptide bond between two amino acids by adding a water molecule across the amide linkage; (II) a restriction endonuclease cleaves a phosphodiester bond in double-stranded DNA using water; (III) a kinase transfers the terminal phosphate of \(\text{ATP}\) onto a serine side chain of a target protein. Which of the described reactions is NOT catalyzed by a hydrolase?

I. The peptidase reaction
II. The endonuclease reaction
III. The kinase reaction', '[{"label": "A", "text": "I only"}, {"label": "B", "text": "III only"}, {"label": "C", "text": "I and III only"}, {"label": "D", "text": "II and III only"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is B because a hydrolase catalyzes a ''hydrolysis reaction'' (EC3) — breaking a bond by adding a water molecule — whereas the kinase transfers a phosphate group from one molecule to another, the defining feature of a transferase (EC2). The IUBMB Enzyme Commission table classifies hydrolases (EC3) as catalyzing ''hydrolysis reactions'' and transferases (EC2) as catalyzing the ''transfer/exchange of group from one molecule to another,'' so the test for each item is whether it adds water (hydrolase) or moves a group (transferase). Item I is a hydrolase: the textbook states that ''Proteolytic enzymes belong to the hydrolase class of enzymes,'' and that the hydrolysis of a peptide bond ''requires the incorporation of a water molecule to separate two amino acids and break the amide bond.'' Item II is also a hydrolase because, as the stem states, the endonuclease cleaves the phosphodiester bond ''using water'' — adding water across a bond is by definition a hydrolysis reaction (EC3). By contrast, item III is a transferase: ''A protein kinase is an enzyme that covalently transfers a terminal phosphate (P) from ATP to a protein,'' and kinases such as hexokinase carry the transferase Enzyme Commission number EC 2.7.1.1, so the kinase is the one reaction NOT catalyzed by a hydrolase. (Choice A) is wrong because the peptidase splits the amide bond by adding water, the defining feature of a hydrolase, and the textbook explicitly places proteolytic enzymes ''in the hydrolase class,'' so item I is a hydrolase rather than the exception. (Choice B) is correct: the kinase moves a phosphate group from ATP onto the protein — the hallmark of a transferase (EC2), confirmed by the EC 2.7.1.1 number for kinases — so the kinase reaction is the one not catalyzed by a hydrolase. (Choice C) is wrong because it incorrectly excludes the peptidase (item I), which uses water to break the peptide bond and is a classic hydrolase; only item III is correct. (Choice D) is wrong because it incorrectly excludes the water-using endonuclease (item II): adding water to cleave the phosphodiester bond is a hydrolysis reaction (EC3), so item II is a hydrolase, while the only non-hydrolase is the phosphate-transferring kinase (item III). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the defining mechanism of each EC class to several described reactions and determine which reaction does not fit the hydrolase definition.', 'hard', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 150)
  RETURNING id
)
