-- Q19 [Active Site, Lock-and-Key & Induced Fit] easy skill1 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Active Site, Lock-and-Key & Induced Fit', 'A researcher mixes purified hexokinase with its normal substrate glucose under conditions that favor binding. As glucose settles into the enzyme''s active site, the two molecules associate before any chemical conversion of glucose occurs. The species that exists at this initial binding step is known as the', '[{"label": "A", "text": "transition-state analog."}, {"label": "B", "text": "enzyme-product complex."}, {"label": "C", "text": "enzyme-inhibitor complex."}, {"label": "D", "text": "enzyme-substrate complex."}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is D because when an enzyme binds its substrate, the two associate at the active site to form an enzyme-substrate (ES) complex before any chemical conversion takes place. OpenStax states, "When an enzyme binds its substrate, it forms an enzyme-substrate complex," and LibreTexts describes the reaction pathway as "The enzyme binds to the substrate to form an enzyme–substrate complex (ES), converts the bound substrate to a bound product via a lower-energy transition state, and finally releases the product." Because glucose has just settled into the active site and has not yet been chemically converted, the species present is the ES complex. (Choice A) A transition-state analog is a stable molecule that chemists synthesize to mimic the reaction''s transition state and act as a tight-binding inhibitor; LibreTexts notes "chemists synthesize stable transition state analogs as potential tight-binding inhibitors," so it is not the normal binding species formed with glucose. (Choice B) The enzyme-product complex (EP) forms only after catalysis, when the bound substrate has been "converts the bound substrate to a bound product," which the stem explicitly states has not yet happened. (Choice C) An enzyme-inhibitor complex forms when an inhibitor rather than the substrate binds the enzyme; LibreTexts refers to "the dissociation constant for the EI complex," but glucose is the normal substrate, not an inhibitor. (Choice D) is correct: the initial binding of enzyme and its normal substrate yields the enzyme-substrate complex. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that the initial enzyme-plus-substrate binding event forms the enzyme-substrate complex.', 'easy', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 1, 75)
  RETURNING id
)
