-- Q20 [Active Site, Lock-and-Key & Induced Fit] medium skill2 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Active Site, Lock-and-Key & Induced Fit', 'A biochemistry student is modeling an enzyme that binds its substrate according to the strict lock-and-key model. In this model, which feature characterizes the enzyme''s active site as the substrate binds?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "The enzyme alters its shape to fit the substrate"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The substrate alters its shape to fit the enzyme"}, {"label": "C", "text": "A preformed complementary site, unchanged on binding"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Binding consumes energy to reshape the site"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is C because in the lock-and-key model the active site is already perfectly complementary to the substrate, so the substrate fits the exact shape of the binding site and binds without any change in the enzyme''s shape. LibreTexts states binding "can occur through a lock-and-key model, in which the reactant fits the exact shape of the enzyme''s binding site" (730-731) and that here "substrates bind to the enzyme''s active site without further modification to the enzyme''s shape" (738-739); the summary calls it a "perfect fit between the enzyme and the substrate" (966). OpenStax likewise describes the lock-and-key view as the enzyme and substrate fitting "together perfectly in one instantaneous step" (8495-8496). (Choice A) describes induced fit, not lock-and-key: induced fit is the model "in which contact between the reactant and the protein changes the protein''s shape" (LibreTexts 732), the reverse of the rigid, preformed site this stem specifies. (Choice B) is wrong because in neither model does the substrate reshape itself to match a static enzyme; lock-and-key holds the active site is already complementary, and induced fit shifts the enzyme, not the substrate (LibreTexts 731-732, 738-739). (Choice C) is correct: the active site is preformed and complementary, and the substrate binds with the enzyme shape unchanged (LibreTexts 731, 738-739, 966). (Choice D) describes the energy-requiring conformational change of induced fit, where "substrate interaction causes the enzyme''s shape to change" (LibreTexts 739); lock-and-key needs no such reshaping. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the defining criteria of two binding models to distinguish lock-and-key from induced fit rather than recall a single fact.', 'medium', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 105)
  RETURNING id
)
