-- Q21 [Active Site, Lock-and-Key & Induced Fit] medium skill1 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Active Site, Lock-and-Key & Induced Fit', 'A researcher studying an enzyme observes that the active site does NOT perfectly complement its substrate beforehand; instead, the two reach an optimal binding arrangement only after they come together. According to the induced-fit model, what accounts for this observation?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Substrate binding changes the enzyme''s shape"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The enzyme stays rigid and unchanged"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The substrate is reshaped while the enzyme stays fixed"}, {"label": "D", "text": "No binding occurs between enzyme and substrate"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is A because, in the induced-fit model, contact between the substrate and the enzyme changes the enzyme''s shape so the active site molds around the substrate, whereas the lock-and-key model assumes a pre-formed perfect fit. LibreTexts describes the induced-fit model as one "in which contact between the reactant and the protein changes the protein''s shape, allowing it to bind," and its Figure 1.2 states that "substrate interaction causes the enzyme''s shape to change to better fit the substrate" (LibreTexts 731-732, 739-740). OpenStax similarly explains that "as the enzyme and substrate come together, their interaction causes a mild shift in the enzyme''s structure that confirms an ideal binding arrangement" (OpenStax 8502-8505). (Choice A) is correct: substrate binding induces a conformational change in the enzyme, molding the active site around the substrate. (Choice B) describes the lock-and-key model, in which substrates bind "without further modification to the enzyme''s shape" (LibreTexts 738-739)—this reverses the relationship the induced-fit model proposes. (Choice C) reverses which partner changes; in induced fit it is the enzyme''s shape that shifts, not a passive enzyme reshaping a fixed substrate. (Choice D) is wrong because binding still occurs—the enzyme-substrate complex forms—it is simply preceded by a conformational change rather than an instantaneous perfect fit. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall how the induced-fit model describes enzyme-substrate binding.', 'medium', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 1, 100)
  RETURNING id
)
