-- Q22 [Active Site, Lock-and-Key & Induced Fit] easy skill1 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Active Site, Lock-and-Key & Induced Fit', 'An enzyme binds only one of several structurally similar molecules in a cell, leaving the others unconverted. This selectivity arises primarily from which feature of the enzyme''s active site?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Its overall molecular weight"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Its number of polypeptide subunits"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The shape and geometry of the active site"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The concentration of available substrate"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is C because the three-dimensional shape of the active site is what physically complements a specific substrate, allowing the enzyme to bind that molecule and not others. According to OpenStax, the unique arrangement of amino acid residues creates a ''jigsaw puzzle-like match between an enzyme and its substrates,'' and ''the best fit results from the shape and the amino acid functional group''s attraction to the substrate'' (OpenStax 8478-8481). LibreTexts states that ''enzyme shape is critical to enzyme function, as it determines the specific binding of a reactant,'' fitting ''the exact shape of the enzyme''s binding site'' in the lock-and-key model (LibreTexts 730-731). (Choice A) Overall molecular weight is a bulk physical property that says nothing about which substrate fits the binding pocket; specificity comes from local shape and residue chemistry, not total mass (OpenStax 8475-8481). (Choice B) The number of subunits describes quaternary organization and does not itself determine which substrate the active site recognizes; the active site forms from folded residues that create the binding geometry (OpenStax 8472-8479). (Choice C) is correct: the complementary shape and geometry of the active site dictate which substrate can bind, which is the structural basis of enzyme specificity (OpenStax 8478-8481; LibreTexts 730-731). (Choice D) Substrate concentration influences how fast a reaction proceeds, not which molecule the active site selects; specificity is set by structure regardless of how much substrate is present (OpenStax 8472-8481). This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that the complementary shape of the active site is the structural cause of enzyme substrate specificity.', 'easy', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 1, 70)
  RETURNING id
)
