-- Q23 [Active Site, Lock-and-Key & Induced Fit] hard skill2 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Active Site, Lock-and-Key & Induced Fit', 'A structural biologist crystallizes an enzyme both free and bound to its substrate and finds that the active site is rigid and unchanged in the free enzyme, but adopts a tighter, complementary shape once the substrate has entered the pocket. Which conclusion(s) does this observation support?

I. The enzyme follows a lock-and-key model, because the bound shapes are complementary.
II. The enzyme follows an induced-fit model, because the conformational change follows substrate binding.
III. The observation supports induced fit over lock-and-key, because shape complementarity is generated by binding rather than preexisting.', '[{"label": "A", "text": "I only"}, {"label": "B", "text": "III only"}, {"label": "C", "text": "I and III only"}, {"label": "D", "text": "II and III only"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is D because the active site is unchanged in the free enzyme and only takes on its complementary shape after the substrate enters, so the conformational change FOLLOWS binding — the defining feature of induced fit. LibreTexts states that in the induced-fit model ''contact between the reactant and the protein changes the protein''s shape, allowing it to bind,'' and that ''substrate interaction causes the enzyme''s shape to change to better fit the substrate'' (LibreTexts 731-732, 738-740). OpenStax describes the same dynamic: ''As the enzyme and substrate come together, their interaction causes a mild shift in the enzyme''s structure that confirms an ideal binding arrangement'' (OpenStax 8495-8505). Statements II and III both describe this binding-induced change, so both are correct. (Choice A) I only is wrong because lock-and-key requires the active site to be ALREADY complementary ''without further modification to the enzyme''s shape'' (LibreTexts 738-740); here the shape changes only after binding, so I is false. (Choice B) III only is incomplete: III is true, but it omits II, which states the same induced-fit conclusion. (Choice C) I and III only pairs a true statement (III) with a false one (I); the final complementary fit is real, but it is generated by binding, not preexisting, so lock-and-key (I) is not supported. (Choice D) is correct: II and III only — both capture that complementarity is induced by binding, the signature of induced fit. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the structural difference between the two binding models to interpret experimental shape data and assign the correct mechanism.', 'hard', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 160)
  RETURNING id
)
