-- Q24 [Active Site, Lock-and-Key & Induced Fit] medium skill2 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Active Site, Lock-and-Key & Induced Fit', 'A researcher compares two enzymes and notes that the first binds its substrate through a rigid pocket whose shape already matches the substrate, whereas in the second the substrate must make contact before the binding pocket molds itself around it. The binding behavior of the second enzyme is best explained by which event on substrate binding?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "The enzyme keeps a fixed, unchanged shape"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The substrate alone reshapes to the enzyme"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Neither molecule changes shape on contact"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Contact reshapes the enzyme around the substrate"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is D because the second enzyme behaves according to the induced-fit model, in which the substrate must contact the enzyme before the binding site forms its complementary shape. LibreTexts contrasts the two models directly: binding can occur "through a lock-and-key model, in which the reactant fits the exact shape of the enzyme''s binding site, or through an induced-fit model, in which contact between the reactant and the protein changes the protein''s shape, allowing it to bind" (lines 731-733). The figure caption reinforces that in the induced-fit model "substrate interaction causes the enzyme''s shape to change to better fit the substrate," whereas in the lock-and-key model substrates bind "without further modification to the enzyme''s shape" (lines 738-740). The second enzyme''s pocket molding itself around the substrate only after contact is the defining signature of induced fit. (Choice D) is correct: in induced fit, "contact between the reactant and the protein changes the protein''s shape, allowing it to bind," so the enzyme is reshaped around the substrate upon contact (lines 732-733, 739-740). (Choice A) is wrong because an enzyme that "keeps a fixed, unchanged shape" describes the lock-and-key model, in which substrates bind "without further modification to the enzyme''s shape" (line 738) — that is the first enzyme, not the second. (Choice B) is wrong because the textbook locates the conformational change in the protein, not the substrate: in induced fit "contact... changes the protein''s shape" (line 732); the model is defined by a shape change in the enzyme, not by the substrate alone reshaping to the enzyme. (Choice C) is wrong because "neither molecule changes shape" denies any conformational change at all, which fits neither model; induced fit specifically requires that "the enzyme''s shape" change "to better fit the substrate" (lines 739-740). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the defining contrast between the lock-and-key and induced-fit models to a described comparison and identify the binding event that characterizes induced fit.', 'medium', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 120)
  RETURNING id
)
