-- Q25 [Enzymes as Catalysts] hard skill2 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Enzymes as Catalysts', 'A reversible enzyme-catalyzed reaction in a sealed tube has been allowed to reach equilibrium, so reactant and product concentrations are no longer changing. A researcher then adds a large additional dose of the same enzyme to the already-equilibrated mixture. Once the system re-stabilizes, the most likely outcome is that', '[{"label": "A", "text": "net product concentration rises"}, {"label": "B", "text": "no net change occurs"}, {"label": "C", "text": "the \\(K_\\text{eq}\\) increases"}, {"label": "D", "text": "\\(\\Delta G\\) becomes more negative"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is B because the reaction is already at equilibrium, and a catalyst speeds the forward and reverse reactions equally, so adding more enzyme produces no net shift in the position of the equilibrium. LibreTexts states that "Enzymes do not affect the thermodynamics of reactions. For reversible reactions (as an example), the equilibrium constant, Keq, is unchanged. What is changed is the rate at which equilibrium is achieved" (lines 26826-26828), and the chapter summary adds that enzymes act "all without changing the overall thermodynamics or equilibrium constant (Keq) of the reaction. Instead, enzymes dramatically increase the rate at which equilibrium is reached by lowering the activation energy" (lines 27698-27699). A catalyst only changes how fast equilibrium is reached, never where it lies; since the mixture is already at equilibrium, there is nothing left to accelerate toward and no net change results. (Choice A) is a tempting partial truth: a catalyst does accelerate the formation of product when a reaction is still approaching equilibrium, but here the system has already arrived, so the equally accelerated reverse reaction exactly offsets the forward one and net product does not rise. (Choice B) is correct: the enzyme leaves the equilibrium position untouched and only the rate at which equilibrium would be reached is affected, so an already-equilibrated system shows no net change. (Choice C) is wrong because \(K_\text{eq}\) is a thermodynamic quantity fixed by the free energies of reactants and products, which the catalyst does not alter; the textbook explicitly states the "equilibrium constant, Keq, is unchanged" (line 26827). (Choice D) is wrong because an enzyme does not change the free energy of reactants or products, so \(\Delta G\) is unchanged, not made more negative; "Enzymes do not affect the thermodynamics of reactions" (line 26826). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the distinction between reaction rate and equilibrium position to a system that is already at equilibrium, rather than recalling the readback fact that \(\Delta G\) is unchanged.', 'hard', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 150)
  RETURNING id
)
